why: master is RED and nobody had claimed it. `okay.TestEffectProvide`
  — "without the grant the same block does not color" — fails on
  origin/master ALONE (verified in a detached worktree at 4dbda8a8: 3
  tests, 1 failed, none of my commits present), so every gate in the
  repository is red until this is fixed.

what it is: a NEGATIVE compile test. It asserts that

    direct[[X] =>> X ! (Reader % Int)] { val env: Int = ask; env }

does NOT compile without the grant. It now compiles. That is not a
stale test — it means `provide` is no longer REQUIRED to colour a
block, and the grant it exists to enforce is currently unenforced.

where it came from: the row-ergonomics range (417b0357..4dbda8a8),
which replaced `Keyed` with `Tag`, added `Refs`, and changed how a
signature's row is derived. Nothing in the queue-swap or
flush-premium lanes touches this area.

also in scope (2): four `import okay.given` gone unused in
  okay-llm/Cut.scala, okay-llm/Structured.scala, okay-http/Nio.scala,
  okay-http/Ws.scala — the same cause (`Keyed` leaving), 75 warning
  lines across JVM/JS/Native, and the repository's gate is meant to
  be warning-free.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  The tempting fix is to delete or weaken the assertion, and that is
  the one move that must not be made without proof. Two outcomes are
  possible and they need different fixes:

  a) the coloring rule genuinely still holds and only the TEST's
     probe is stale — the row spelling, the operation name or the
     compile-check helper changed under it. Then the test is
     repaired to probe the real rule, and it must FAIL against a
     deliberately ungranted block before I believe the repair.
  b) the rule does NOT hold: some given now colours the block without
     a grant. Then the MACHINERY is fixed and the test stands as
     written.

  I will tell them apart before touching either, by finding what
  actually satisfies the ungranted block today — not by reading the
  diff and guessing. No failing test, no fix: whatever I change, the
  ungranted block must be shown to be accepted first and refused
  after.

predict: (b) — a given widened in the Tag/Refs work and now supplies
  the policy unasked. Recorded so being wrong is visible.
